#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bin/heimdall-relay-client -- publishes hmd's `hmd ui` state through the
Cloudflare relay (see hmdapp's docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-relay.md and
docs/superpowers/specs/relay/INVARIANTS.md -- both read-only inputs from the
hmdapp repo, never edited here) and accepts a `send-message` command back from
the paired phone -- and a `decide` command, the phone's sealed DENY of a pending
risky action (A4, deny-only round: see `_decide`; nothing else about an action can
be decided from the phone, and "allow" is refused for every request) -- and, to an hmd that lists
`push-v1`, the phone's sealed push registration (see PUSH REGISTRATION below) -- and, to an hmd that
lists `login-v1`, the phone's sealed remote Claude Code login (see REMOTE CLAUDE CODE LOGIN below).

Spawned by `bin/heimdall-app connect --relay <url>` as a sibling process to a
local `hmd ui`; this process owns the ONE relay session for its lifetime and is
torn down by `hmd app disconnect` sending it SIGTERM.

Zero second state-collection path: state is read directly from
sentinels/hmd-ui.py's collect_state/digest_of/StateCache (imported by path --
the same convention that module itself uses for bin/lib/*.py, see
`_load_module` below) -- never over a second HTTP hop to a locally running
`hmd ui`. `--ui-port` is carried only for the transport dict (so a phone-side
viewer can show which local port `hmd ui` is actually bound to); the state
read path never opens a socket to it.

SEND-ON-CHANGE: a state frame follows the change, not a timer. The cache's
poller re-collects the moment a watched source moves (StateCache.run) and
wakes StateCache.wait_for_change; run_state_loop blocks there, lets a burst
settle for STATE_DEBOUNCE_S (never longer than STATE_DEBOUNCE_MAX_S since its
first change), then sends ONE frame carrying the newest state. A frame is
still sent only when the digest differs from the last one sent (INV-22), a
re-bind forces one regardless (§7c), and the loop re-checks stop_event / that
re-bind at least every STATE_WAIT_SLICE_S.

SESSION-KEY BOOTSTRAP (matches hmdapp's RelayTransport / fake-hmd.mjs exactly
-- see hmdapp's docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-relay-client-fixes.md Section 1;
this section previously documented a "Wave-1 hello" handshake the real app
never implements, retired 2026-09-24):
    The relay's `GET /session/:id/stream` pushes an unencrypted `device_bound`
    control frame (sender "relay") down this leg once the phone has completed
    pairing -- `payload` on THIS leg is `{"device_pubkey": <b64 X25519
    pubkey>, "bound_at": <epoch seconds>}` (the phone leg's own `device_bound`
    carries a different, unrelated payload -- `{device_token, exp}` -- never
    seen here). There is no second frame, no "hello": as soon as
    `device_bound` arrives, this client decodes `device_pubkey`, calls
    `derive_session_key(hmd_priv, device_pub, session_id)` (INV-11:
    HKDF-SHA256(ECDH(hmd_priv, device_pub), salt=session_id,
    info="hmd-relay-v1")), and is immediately ready to open `command` frames
    -- no ack is sent for `device_bound` itself (unlike a real `command`
    frame, it is a control frame, INV-20). `device_bound` may arrive more
    than once per process lifetime (e.g. after a stream reconnect); the
    session key is latched on the FIRST occurrence and never re-derived
    after (§9, audit #1). A repeat carrying the SAME device_pubkey is the
    expected reconnect case -- treated as success, never as an error, and
    still forces the next state tick to re-publish regardless of digest
    (§7c) -- but a repeat carrying a DIFFERENT device_pubkey is never
    trusted or adopted: a loud `error` event, key and pairing state left
    untouched, since silently accepting it would let a relay bug (or a
    malicious relay) rebind an already-paired session onto an
    attacker-controlled device key.
    A `device_bound` whose `device_pubkey` is missing or fails to decode
    (INV-33: a relay-injected or malformed control frame must never be
    silently trusted) is a loud `error` event, ignored -- never crashes,
    never derives a key, never marks the session paired. A `command` frame
    from sender "device" arriving before any `device_bound` has succeeded is
    likewise a loud `error` event, ignored, never a crash on a null session
    key.

STDOUT CONTRACT: newline-delimited JSON, one compact (sorted-key) object per
line, flushed immediately -- the ONLY channel `hmd app connect --relay` reads
to drive its own UI; nothing else is ever written to stdout.
    {"event":"pair_init","qr":{"v":1,"relay":..,"session_id":..,
     "pairing_code":..,"exp":..,"hmd_pubkey":..},"exp":..}
        `qr`, serialized compact+sorted (which this whole line already is), IS
        the exact QR payload string heimdall-app renders (Decision 7.2 shape).
    {"event":"device_bound","bound_at":..}            session key derived --
                                                       may repeat across a
                                                       stream reconnect
    {"event":"state_sent","seq":N,"bytes":B,"delivered":bool}
    {"event":"device_caps","caps":[..]}               the phone's capability set
                                                       (from its latest sealed
                                                       `resync`; [] after a
                                                       device_bound), sorted, logged
                                                       when it CHANGES. See FRAME
                                                       COMPRESSION AND RESYNC below
    {"event":"command","action":"send-message"|"decide"|"resync"|"register_push"|
     "unregister_push"|"app_state"|"login_start"|"login_code"|"login_cancel"|..,
     "ok":bool,"detail":..}
        one line per inbound command frame that reaches decrypted-payload
        handling (a `keepalive` control frame is always silent -- never
        logged, not even at this level); any action other than the nine
        named (the three login_* only while bin/lib/companion_cc_login.py
        imported) is acked `not-implemented`. Never the token, ref or label
        of a push registration (see PUSH REGISTRATION below), never a login
        command's params (see REMOTE CLAUDE CODE LOGIN below)
    {"event":"ack_sent","of_seq":N,"seq":S,"id":..|null,"attempt":K,"ms":N,
     "ok":bool,"delivered":bool}
        one line per POST ATTEMPT of the ack answering a command (so every
        `command` line is followed by at least one): of_seq is the command's
        device seq, seq this ack frame's own hmd-side seq (identical on every
        attempt -- see ACK RETRY below), id the inbox record id the ack
        carries (null when nothing was written), ms how long this attempt took,
        ok whether the relay gave a usable answer (HTTP 200) and delivered the
        relay's own flag for it (false too when the POST failed)
    {"event":"connect","family":"ipv4"|"ipv6","ms":N,"dns_ms":N,"attempts":N,
     "for":"pair"|"stream"|"frames"|"revoke"|"request"}
        one line per NEW relay connection (/pair/init, /stream and /revoke open
        their own every time; POST /frames only when it has no live connection
        to reuse -- see FRAME CONNECTIONS below): which address family won, how
        long resolving plus connecting took (dns_ms the resolving part of it),
        and how many addresses had to be started to get there -- 1 is the
        normal case, 2+ means an earlier address was too slow or failed and a
        later one was raced in. See CONNECTING below
    {"event":"post","ms":N,"reused":bool}
        one line per POST /frames the relay ANSWERED (whatever the HTTP
        status): ms how long the whole POST took -- the connect and TLS
        handshake included when it had to open a connection, and the retry on a
        replaced stale connection included -- reused whether it went out on a
        connection that was already open. See FRAME CONNECTIONS below
    {"event":"stream_drop","retry_ms":N[,"reason":..]}
                                                       GET stream disconnected;
                                                       reconnecting after N ms.
                                                       reason "idle": silent
                                                       past
                                                       HMD_RELAY_STREAM_IDLE_S.
                                                       reason "overflow": a
                                                       line or this
                                                       connection's running
                                                       total exceeded
                                                       STREAM_READ_CAP_BYTES;
                                                       carries extra
                                                       line_bytes/total_bytes/
                                                       cap_bytes fields. reason
                                                       "rotate": planned self-
                                                       rotation at
                                                       HMD_RELAY_STREAM_ROTATE_S
                                                       -- retry_ms always 0, no
                                                       backoff. reason "closed":
                                                       clean EOF with idle,
                                                       overflow and rotate all
                                                       ruled out -- likely the
                                                       relay's own stream-
                                                       lifetime bound, possibly
                                                       after a relay deploy; a
                                                       WARN-level error event
                                                       names this immediately
                                                       before the stream_drop.
                                                       reason absent: a genuine
                                                       local read fault (socket
                                                       / HTTP exception)
    {"event":"session_ended","reason":..}             terminal; process exits 0
    {"event":"error","detail":..}                     non-fatal unless followed
                                                       by one of the two exit
                                                       paths below
    {"event":"login","phase":"start"|"code"|"result","id":..|null,"ok":bool,"detail":..|null}
        remote Claude Code login (login-v1, bin/lib/companion_cc_login.py): one line per start
        attempt (id null, ok false and the refusal as detail when refused), per code submitted
        (ok false + detail when refused) and per result (ok, detail one of the spec's closed
        result.detail strings). Nothing but these fields: never the URL, the code or an account

Never printed, logged, or written to disk: `relay_session_token`, the derived
session key, either private key, or any command/state plaintext.

CONNECTING (HMD_RELAY_IP_FAMILY=auto|4|6, default auto; hmdapp's
docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-relay-ipv6-stall.md): a connect to the relay over IPv6
can black-hole on some networks (SYN dropped, connect() hangs ~10 s) while the
resolver hands IPv6 back FIRST -- on the operator's Mac about one request in
five stalled that way, which is how hmd->relay POSTs (state frames AND acks)
missed the phone's 10 s ack window. Every connection -- /pair/init, GET
/stream, POST /frames, /revoke -- is therefore opened by _create_connection,
an RFC 8305 "Happy Eyeballs" connect: the addresses are ordered IPv4 first
(families interleaved), the first is started at once, each next one
HE_STAGGER_S (250 ms) after the previous -- or immediately when an attempt
fails -- and the first to connect wins; the rest are abandoned. IPv4 first is
a deliberate departure from RFC 8305's IPv6-first default: the relay is
dual-stack Cloudflare, IPv4 to it is reliable, and never starting a flaky path
is cheaper than racing it. TLS is untouched: http.client wraps the winning
socket with server_hostname = the relay's NAME, so SNI and certificate
verification never see the address. HMD_RELAY_IP_FAMILY=4 or 6 restricts the
lookup to that family, with no fallback to the other; any other value is
reported as an `error` event and treated as auto.

FRAME CONNECTIONS (docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-zero-lag-sync.md Ask 3): POST
/frames used to open a new TCP+TLS connection for every frame, and about a
quarter of those needed a second Happy Eyeballs address -- the whole p95 of
every state frame and every ack. Each SENDER now keeps ONE persistent HTTP/1.1
connection of its own (state frames on one, acks on the other, neither shared
with GET /stream), each behind its own lock, so a slow state POST can never
hold up an ack. A connection is (re)opened, through the same Happy Eyeballs
connect as before, only when there is none or a request on it failed, and one
that raised is closed, never reused. TCP keepalive keeps a quiet connection's
NAT mapping alive and turns a path that died silently into a socket error
within about a minute, rather than into a POST that waits out its whole
timeout. A failure on a REUSED connection is nearly always one the relay or a
middlebox closed while it idled, so the SAME sealed envelope (same seq, nonce
and ciphertext: the phone dedupes on seq, so a duplicate is harmless) is
POSTed once more, at once, on a fresh connection, inside the same timeout.
Not retried here: a timeout (it spent the whole budget; the caller's own retry
-- the ack retry below, the next state tick -- takes over on a fresh
connection) and a failure on a connection opened for that very POST (nothing
about it was stale).

STATE FRAMES (Ask 4): a state frame's digest (INV-22) is recorded as sent only
once its POST got an answer from the relay. A POST that got none -- network
error, timeout, non-200, a frame over the envelope cap refused locally --
leaves the digest as it was, so the next tick sends the then-current state
again under a fresh seq, after a backoff of 250 ms, 1 s, 2 s and then 5 s that
the first answered POST resets. delivered=false (no phone connected) IS an
answer and is not retried: a (re)bind forces the resend. The relay client
shares ONE hmd-side seq counter between state frames and acks, and the phone
drops a frame whose seq a newer one has already passed, so an ack must never
be overtaken: while an ack is in flight (its retries included) no state frame
takes a seq. The converse is accepted -- an ack takes a seq above a state
frame that is still in flight and may land first -- but then that state frame
is not recorded as sent either, and the next tick sends it again.

ACK RETRY (HMD_RELAY_ACK_WINDOW_S, default 8): the phone gives a command 10 s
to be acked, and an ack whose POST failed used to be lost for good. An ack POST
that gets no usable answer (network error, timeout, non-200) -- or whose answer
is delivered=false: the relay has no phone socket right now, an ack is never
stored, and the phone may well be reconnecting inside its window -- is
re-POSTed: the SAME sealed envelope, same seq, nonce and ciphertext, never a
re-seal: the app drops a frame whose seq it already accepted before it even
decrypts, so a retry after a lost RESPONSE is a harmless duplicate, not a
second ack. At most 3 attempts in all, 0.25 s then 0.5 s apart, each bounded
to 3 s (a state frame keeps the 15 s connect timeout). A RETRY starts only if
it can start inside HMD_RELAY_ACK_WINDOW_S of the command's arrival -- the
app's 10 s minus transit -- and is capped to what is left of it; the first
attempt is always made, since a late ack beats none and the app accepts one.
No later frame can overtake the retries (see STATE FRAMES: the app would drop
an ack whose seq a newer frame has already passed). Every attempt is visible
in ack_sent, a delivered=false answer too (ok true, delivered false). Backoff
waits on stop_event, so a shutdown is never delayed by a retry.

FRAME COMPRESSION AND RESYNC (the FINAL wire of hmdapp's docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-zero-lag-sync.md
Ask 5, section 5; hmdapp's docs/HANDBACK-FROM-HEIMDALL-zlib-frames.md records what hmd does with
it; codec in bin/lib/hmd_relay_e2e.py). Capabilities travel in-band, hmd first: EVERY `state`
frame's plaintext is `{"state":{...},"caps":["resync","z-zlib"]}` (E2E.hmd_caps()). A phone that
sees `resync` sends a sealed command `{"action":"resync","params":{"last_seq":N,"digest":"<sha256
hex>","caps":[..]}}`; hmd acks it ok, keeps its `caps` as the phone's capability set (replaced by
every resync, forgotten on every device_bound), and -- when `digest` is not the digest of the
state hmd last sent -- forces a full state frame on the next tick, the same re-send device_bound
forces. Only while the phone's set holds "z-zlib" is a state frame sealed as `{"z":"zlib","d":
"<base64 of zlib.compress(that plain plaintext)>"}`, compressed BEFORE the seal. Plain is the
default and the fallback: no resync yet, a frame under E2E.COMPRESS_MIN_BYTES, one the envelope
would not shrink, `ack` frames (never compressed), a python without zlib. Only a sealed command
that opened under the session key can set the phone's capabilities -- never the relay's
unencrypted `device_bound`.

PUSH REGISTRATION (hmdapp's docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-push-notifications.md PN2 and section 5 of its spec,
docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-03-push-notifications.md; bin/lib/companion_push_store.py owns every rule and
keeps <repo>/.heimdall/app/push.json). Every state frame lists `push-v1` in its caps unless the operator set
HMD_PUSH=0 or the store failed to load. A phone that sees it may send three sealed commands, each acked
`{"ok":..,"of_seq":N[,"detail":..]}` plus the keys named here:
    register_push    {"v":1,"provider":"expo","token":..,"platform":"ios"|"android","ref":"<16 hex>",
                      "label":..,"events":[..]}        ack ok adds "ref" and "events" (sorted)
    unregister_push  {"ref":"<16 hex>"}                ack ok adds "ref" and "removed":bool
    app_state        {"state":"active"|"background"}
A refusal's `detail` is push-disabled | bad-params | bad-version | bad-provider | bad-token | bad-platform |
bad-ref | bad-label | bad-events | bad-state | write-failed. The Expo token is a secret: never printed, never
logged, never in the status file, never echoed in an ack. Registrations left by an EARLIER session are
cleared when this session latches its key (its first device_bound); a repeat device_bound leaves them alone.

REMOTE CLAUDE CODE LOGIN (capability `login-v1`; hmdapp's docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-remote-cc-login.md):
`caps` also lists `login-v1` whenever bin/lib/companion_cc_login.py imported. A phone that lists it in its
resync gets `state.login` ({enabled, cc, request, result}, sealed with the rest of the state) and may send
the sealed commands `login_start` {kind}, `login_code` {id, code} and `login_cancel` {id}; each ack carries
the manager's refusal code in `detail` (see _login_command). Off by default: the operator enables it at
the laptop with `hmd app remote-login on`. The login code exists only inside the sealed command and one
write to a PTY; the `command` event never carries params and the `login` event never carries the URL,
the code or an account.

EXIT CODES: 0 clean end (session_ended, or a signal-driven revoke); 2 usage;
11 no E2E backend (`e2e_available()` false, or bin/lib/hmd_relay_e2e.py failed
to import -- both mean the same thing: refuse to start a plaintext relay
session); 12 relay unreachable or `/pair/init` did not return 200; 1 an
unexpected local fault outside all four of the above (e.g. sentinels/hmd-ui.py
itself failed to import).

STATUS FILE (default <repo>/.heimdall/app/relay.json, atomic 0600 write, 0700
dir, rewritten on every state change -- never holds a token or key):
    {session_id, relay, paired, bound_at, last_seq, frames_sent,
     last_delivered, last_command_at, started_at, pid, event_log}

EVENT LOG (HMD_RELAY_EVENT_LOG; default <repo>/.heimdall/app/relay-events.jsonl,
atomic per-line append, 0600, 0700 dir; set to "" to disable -- no file is ever
created in that case): a durable copy of every line this process ever writes
to stdout (see STDOUT CONTRACT above -- unchanged, byte-for-byte), each with
one added "ts" (ISO-8601 UTC, millisecond precision) field the stdout copy
omits. Same secrets discipline as STATUS FILE above -- never a token, key, or
command/state plaintext. Rotates at EVENT_LOG_MAX_BYTES (4 MiB),
single-generation (path -> path.1, clobbering any previous .1 -- same
mechanism as bin/lib/companion_ui_inbox.py's _rotate_if_oversized). A write
failure (unwritable dir, disk full, ...) disables the log for the rest of
this process's life and prints exactly one stderr notice -- never blocks or
crashes the client, and never retried (a second failure would otherwise mean
a second notice, defeating the point).
"""

import argparse
import functools
import http.client
import itertools
import json
import os
import queue
import signal
import socket
import ssl
import sys
import threading
import time
import urllib.parse
from importlib.util import module_from_spec, spec_from_file_location

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
LIB_DIR = os.path.join(HERE, "lib")
SENTINELS_DIR = os.path.normpath(os.path.join(HERE, "..", "sentinels"))


def _load_module(name, path):
    """Import a sibling module by path -- the same convention sentinels/hmd-ui.py
    uses for its own bin/lib/*.py imports (see that file's `_load_module`).
    Returns None on any failure (missing file, syntax error, ...) rather than
    raising, so a caller can degrade explicitly instead of the whole process
    dying on import."""
    try:
        spec = spec_from_file_location(name, path)
        mod = module_from_spec(spec)
        spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
        return mod
    except Exception:
        return None


UI = _load_module("hmd_ui", os.path.join(SENTINELS_DIR, "hmd-ui.py"))
INBOX = _load_module("companion_ui_inbox", os.path.join(LIB_DIR, "companion_ui_inbox.py"))
DECISIONS = _load_module("companion_ui_decisions", os.path.join(LIB_DIR, "companion_ui_decisions.py"))
E2E = _load_module("hmd_relay_e2e", os.path.join(LIB_DIR, "hmd_relay_e2e.py"))
PUSH_STORE = _load_module("companion_push_store", os.path.join(LIB_DIR, "companion_push_store.py"))
LOGIN = _load_module("companion_cc_login", os.path.join(LIB_DIR, "companion_cc_login.py"))

# HMD_RELAY_MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES (default 1048576 == 1 MiB): the largest sealed
# envelope this client will POST to /session/:id/frames -- one named constant,
# matching the relay's and hmdapp's own INV-16 cap. Raised from the original
# 128 KiB on 2026-09-24: the app side reported this client silently dropping
# outbound state frames once a real hmd state payload (~136 KB) exceeded the
# old cap, while the relay/app had already moved to 1 MiB. A frame still over
# cap after this raise is refused HERE, locally, before ever reaching the
# network -- loudly, via an `error` event naming both the frame's size and the
# cap (see send_frame_envelope below), never a silent drop.
try:
    MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES = int(os.environ.get("HMD_RELAY_MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES", "1048576"))
except (TypeError, ValueError):
    MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES = 1048576
# HMD_RELAY_BACKOFF_BASE_MS (default 2000ms): starting reconnect backoff after
# a dropped/failed stream, doubling on each consecutive failure up to
# BACKOFF_CAP_MS and resetting to this base on the next successful (200)
# reconnect. Overridable so a test can drive a fast, timestamp-verifiable
# backoff assertion (docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-relay-client-fixes.md Section
# 15) without waiting out the real default. BACKOFF_CAP_MS stays a bare
# literal on purpose -- an existing test asserts its exact literal-assignment
# source form.
try:
    BACKOFF_BASE_MS = int(os.environ.get("HMD_RELAY_BACKOFF_BASE_MS", "2000"))
except (TypeError, ValueError):
    BACKOFF_BASE_MS = 2000
BACKOFF_CAP_MS = 30000
CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S = 15
STREAM_TIMEOUT_S = None  # unbounded connect + response-header wait for GET
                          # /stream -- the long-lived body that follows has
                          # its own bound, STREAM_IDLE_S below; this constant
                          # only ever governed (and still only governs) the
                          # phase before the first line of that body exists.

# HMD_RELAY_STREAM_IDLE_S (default 60s -- three missed keepalives at the
# relay's own 20s cadence): bounds silence on an ALREADY-OPEN GET /stream
# body. A dead SessionDO can leave this client's chunked response open and
# empty forever otherwise (docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-relay-client-fixes.md
# Section 6). On expiry: abort the read, reconnect through the normal retry
# path, emit stream_drop with reason "idle" -- see _stream_loop.
try:
    STREAM_IDLE_S = float(os.environ.get("HMD_RELAY_STREAM_IDLE_S", "60"))
except (TypeError, ValueError):
    STREAM_IDLE_S = 60.0

# Bounds the GET /stream reader against a hostile or malformed peer
# (docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-relay-client-fixes.md Section 13): both a single
# line and the running total read on one stream connection are capped at the
# same INV-16 envelope size, reused rather than duplicated -- a line on this
# stream IS an envelope, and real inbound traffic on this leg (device_bound
# once, a keepalive control frame every ~20s, occasional small command
# frames) never approaches it, so this never trips in normal operation. +1
# over MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES: the sender's own cap (send_frame_envelope below)
# measures the serialized envelope body with no trailing newline, but the
# NDJSON wire format adds exactly one delimiter byte per line that accounting
# never counts -- without the +1, a legitimately maximally-sized envelope
# would read back as a false overflow. On overflow: abort the read, drop the
# stream, reconnect through the normal retry path, emit stream_drop with
# reason "overflow" and the offending byte counts -- see _stream_loop.
STREAM_READ_CAP_BYTES = MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES + 1

# HMD_RELAY_STREAM_ROTATE_S (default 540s == 9 minutes): plan a self-initiated
# stream close+reopen before the relay's own stream-lifetime bound (10
# minutes, docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-relay-client-fixes.md Section 16) ever
# fires -- so THIS client is usually the one ending the stream, belt-and-
# braces against the one failure class STREAM_IDLE_S alone cannot catch: a
# Durable Object generation rollover (any relay deploy) orphaning the old
# generation's keepalive timer in a now-unreachable isolate, which keeps
# writing a keepalive forever and so never goes idle. No jitter -- a fixed
# interval is what makes HMD_RELAY_STREAM_ROTATE_S=<small N> deterministically
# testable. On rotation: close and reopen immediately, no backoff (this is
# planned, not a fault) -- emit stream_drop reason "rotate", retry_ms 0 -- see
# _stream_loop.
try:
    STREAM_ROTATE_S = float(os.environ.get("HMD_RELAY_STREAM_ROTATE_S", "540"))
except (TypeError, ValueError):
    STREAM_ROTATE_S = 540.0

# HMD_RELAY_IP_FAMILY (default auto): which address families _create_connection
# may use -- auto (IPv4 first, IPv6 raced in as the fallback), or 4 / 6 (that
# family only, no fallback). See CONNECTING in the module docstring. An
# unrecognised value falls back to auto; main() reports it as an `error` event
# once the event log exists (this runs at import, before it can).
_IP_FAMILY_ENV = os.environ.get("HMD_RELAY_IP_FAMILY", "auto").strip().lower() or "auto"
IP_FAMILY_VALID = _IP_FAMILY_ENV in ("auto", "4", "6")
IP_FAMILY = _IP_FAMILY_ENV if IP_FAMILY_VALID else "auto"
# RFC 8305 section 5, "Connection Attempt Delay" (250 ms recommended): how long
# one address gets to connect before the next is started alongside it.
HE_STAGGER_S = 0.25

# Ack retry (see ACK RETRY in the module docstring). ACK_WINDOW_S is how long
# after a command's arrival a RETRY of its ack may still START -- the phone
# gives a command 10 s, minus transit. ACK_ATTEMPT_TIMEOUT_S bounds one ack POST
# (connect + request + response; a state frame keeps CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S).
# ACK_RETRY_DELAYS_S are the waits between attempts 1->2 and 2->3 -- 3 attempts
# in all, the doc's "<=3 tries within ~8 s". ACK_MIN_ATTEMPT_S is the floor a
# window-capped retry's timeout is never squeezed below: a retry given a few
# milliseconds could only fail, and fail to no purpose.
try:
    ACK_WINDOW_S = float(os.environ.get("HMD_RELAY_ACK_WINDOW_S", "8"))
except (TypeError, ValueError):
    ACK_WINDOW_S = 8.0
if not 0 <= ACK_WINDOW_S <= 3600:  # also catches nan
    ACK_WINDOW_S = 8.0
ACK_ATTEMPT_TIMEOUT_S = 3.0
ACK_RETRY_DELAYS_S = (0.25, 0.5)
ACK_MIN_ATTEMPT_S = 0.5

# State retry (see STATE FRAMES in the module docstring): a state POST that got no
# usable answer is re-sent by the next tick, after the next of these waits -- 250 ms,
# 1 s, 2 s, then held at 5 s -- the first answered POST resetting it to the start.
STATE_RETRY_BACKOFF_S = (0.25, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0)

# TCP keepalive on a persistent frame connection (see FRAME CONNECTIONS): the first
# probe after KEEPALIVE_IDLE_S of silence, then one every KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL_S, the
# connection given up after KEEPALIVE_PROBES unanswered -- a dead path surfaces as a
# socket error in about a minute instead of as a POST waiting out its whole timeout.
KEEPALIVE_IDLE_S = 30
KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL_S = 10
KEEPALIVE_PROBES = 3

# HMD_RELAY_EVENT_LOG (docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-relay-send-ack.md "New ask"):
# durable append-only NDJSON mirror of every line emit() writes to stdout, so
# a backgrounded session (stdout usually discarded, see cmd_connect_relay's
# --bg path in bin/heimdall-app) still leaves a record a phone-side session
# can read after the fact. None == unset (use the default path under --repo,
# resolved once configure_event_log() runs); "" == explicitly disabled, no
# file ever created; anything else overrides the path outright.
_EVENT_LOG_ENV = os.environ.get("HMD_RELAY_EVENT_LOG")
EVENT_LOG_MAX_BYTES = 4 * 1024 * 1024  # 4 MiB, single-generation rotation to .1

_event_log_path = None       # resolved by configure_event_log(); None before
                              # that call AND whenever logging is disabled --
                              # both cases mean "emit() writes nothing extra"
_event_log_notified = False  # true once the one allowed stderr failure
                              # notice (see _append_event_log) has fired

# emit() is called from two threads with no prior coordination between them
# (main()'s stream_thread running RelayClient._stream_loop, and the main
# thread's own run_state_loop periodic tick -- see main() below), and unlike
# self.lock above (an INSTANCE lock guarding frame-sequencing state, unrelated
# to this), nothing previously serialized concurrent emit() calls -- each
# thread's stdout write was already syscall-atomic on its own (one write() per
# line, well under PIPE_BUF), so lines never interleaved character-by-
# character, but two threads' EVENTS could still land in either order. That
# was harmless when stdout was the only output. Now that every event is ALSO
# durably logged (a second, separate write() to a second fd), the two writes
# making up one emit() call are no longer a single syscall -- without a lock,
# thread A's stdout write and thread B's stdout write could happen in one
# order while thread A's log write and thread B's log write happen in the
# OTHER order, leaving stdout and the durable log genuinely disagreeing about
# what happened first. This lock makes each emit() call's [stdout write, log
# append] pair atomic relative to every other emit() call, so the log is
# always a faithful mirror of stdout's own order -- whatever that order turns
# out to be -- rather than an independently-racing second record of it.
_emit_lock = threading.Lock()


def configure_event_log(repo_root):
    """Resolve the durable event-log path from HMD_RELAY_EVENT_LOG and
    `repo_root` into module state -- emit() is a bare function called before
    any RelayClient exists (see main(), and the pair_init/e2e-unavailable
    error paths above it), so this can't live on an instance the way
    status_path does. Idempotent; main() calls it exactly once, right after
    --repo is parsed, so even the earliest emit() calls are covered."""
    global _event_log_path
    if _EVENT_LOG_ENV == "":
        _event_log_path = None
        return
    _event_log_path = _EVENT_LOG_ENV or os.path.join(repo_root, ".heimdall", "app", "relay-events.jsonl")


def _rotate_event_log_if_oversized(path):
    """Single-generation rotation: path -> path.1 (clobbering any previous
    .1) once `path` is already >= EVENT_LOG_MAX_BYTES, so the next append
    starts a fresh file -- identical mechanism to
    bin/lib/companion_ui_inbox.py's _rotate_if_oversized, minus its flock
    (that file has multiple writers; this one has exactly one -- a single
    relay-client process owns its own event log for its whole lifetime)."""
    try:
        if os.path.getsize(path) < EVENT_LOG_MAX_BYTES:
            return
    except OSError:
        return
    os.replace(path, path + ".1")


def _event_log_timestamp():
    now = time.time()
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S", time.gmtime(now)) + ".%03dZ" % int(now % 1 * 1000)


def _append_event_log(obj):
    """Best-effort durable copy of one emit() line, plus an ISO-8601 UTC "ts"
    field the stdout copy never carries (STDOUT CONTRACT is unchanged).
    Never raises and never blocks the caller on failure: a broken log
    (unwritable dir, disk full, ...) disables itself for the rest of this
    process's life and prints exactly one stderr notice, then keeps going --
    persisting events is a debugging convenience, not a correctness gate the
    actual relay session depends on. Broad except-and-report is deliberate,
    matching this file's own convention at every non-critical I/O boundary
    (e.g. run_state_loop's per-tick try/except, or _atomic_write_json's
    try/except above)."""
    global _event_log_path, _event_log_notified
    if _event_log_path is None:
        return
    try:
        d = os.path.dirname(_event_log_path)
        os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        os.chmod(d, 0o700)
        _rotate_event_log_if_oversized(_event_log_path)
        record = dict(obj)
        record["ts"] = _event_log_timestamp()
        line = json.dumps(record, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n"
        fd = os.open(_event_log_path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_APPEND, 0o600)
        os.chmod(_event_log_path, 0o600)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(line)
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
    except Exception as e:
        _event_log_path = None
        if not _event_log_notified:
            _event_log_notified = True
            sys.stderr.write(
                "heimdall-relay-client: event log disabled after a write failure "
                "(%s) -- continuing without it\n" % e)


def emit(obj):
    with _emit_lock:
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(obj, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n")
        sys.stdout.flush()
        _append_event_log(obj)


def _atomic_write_json(path, obj):
    d = os.path.dirname(path)
    os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(d, 0o700)
    tmp = path + ".tmp-%d" % os.getpid()
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(obj, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":")))
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        os.chmod(tmp, 0o600)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except Exception:
        os.unlink(tmp)
        raise


def _split_relay(url):
    parsed = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url or "")
    if parsed.scheme not in ("http", "https") or not parsed.hostname:
        return None
    return parsed


_FAMILY_NAME = {socket.AF_INET: "ipv4", socket.AF_INET6: "ipv6"}


def _ordered_addrinfo(host, port):
    """Every address `host` resolves to, in the order to try them: IPv4 first,
    then IPv6, families interleaved (v4, v6, v4, v6, ...) so a fallback address
    of EITHER family is never more than one stagger away. getaddrinfo's own
    order is deliberately not kept -- it hands IPv6 back first, which is exactly
    the order that stalled (see CONNECTING in the module docstring).
    HMD_RELAY_IP_FAMILY=4|6 narrows the lookup itself to one family."""
    want = {"4": socket.AF_INET, "6": socket.AF_INET6}.get(IP_FAMILY, socket.AF_UNSPEC)
    infos = socket.getaddrinfo(host, port, want, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
    v4 = [i for i in infos if i[0] == socket.AF_INET]
    v6 = [i for i in infos if i[0] == socket.AF_INET6]
    return [i for pair in itertools.zip_longest(v4, v6) for i in pair if i is not None]


def _create_connection(address, timeout, source_address=None, label="request"):
    """socket.create_connection(), but RFC 8305 "Happy Eyeballs" instead of one
    address after another -- see CONNECTING in the module docstring for why.

    The first address (_ordered_addrinfo) is started at once and each next one
    HE_STAGGER_S after the previous, or immediately when an attempt fails; every
    attempt keeps running while later ones start, and the first to connect wins.
    `timeout` is ONE budget for the whole race (None: none of ours -- the OS's
    own connect limit still ends each attempt), not one per address, and
    becomes the winner's I/O timeout. Attempts are blocking connects on daemon
    threads, so a stalled one can never delay process exit; one that is still
    connecting when the race is decided closes its own socket the moment it
    finishes. On success emits a `connect` event; on failure raises ONE OSError
    naming every address tried and why, so a caller's `except OSError` keeps
    working exactly as it did for socket.create_connection."""
    host, port = address
    started = time.monotonic()
    infos = _ordered_addrinfo(host, port)
    resolved = time.monotonic()
    if not infos:
        raise OSError("no IPv4/IPv6 address found for %s:%s" % (host, port))
    deadline = None if timeout is None else resolved + timeout

    results = queue.Queue()
    lock = threading.Lock()
    settled = []  # non-empty once the race is decided; guarded by `lock`

    def attempt(info, attempt_timeout):
        family, socktype, proto, _canonname, sockaddr = info
        sock = None
        try:
            sock = socket.socket(family, socktype, proto)
            sock.settimeout(attempt_timeout)
            if source_address:
                sock.bind(source_address)
            sock.connect(sockaddr)
        except Exception as e:  # reported to the race below, never swallowed
            if sock is not None:
                sock.close()
            outcome = (info, None, e)
        else:
            outcome = (info, sock, None)
        with lock:
            if settled and outcome[1] is not None:
                outcome[1].close()  # decided while this one was still connecting
                return
            results.put(outcome)

    errors = []    # (info, exception) for every attempt that failed
    inflight = []  # infos started and not yet reported back
    launched = 0
    next_launch = resolved
    winner = None
    try:
        while winner is None:
            now = time.monotonic()
            if deadline is not None and now >= deadline:
                break
            if launched < len(infos) and (not inflight or now >= next_launch):
                info = infos[launched]
                launched += 1
                try:
                    threading.Thread(target=attempt, name="relay-connect", daemon=True,
                                     args=(info, None if deadline is None else deadline - now)).start()
                except RuntimeError as e:  # out of threads: this address failed to start
                    errors.append((info, e))
                else:
                    inflight.append(info)
                    next_launch = now + HE_STAGGER_S
                continue
            if not inflight:
                break  # every address has failed
            wait = None if launched >= len(infos) else max(next_launch - now, 0.0)
            if deadline is not None:
                wait = deadline - now if wait is None else min(wait, deadline - now)
            try:
                info, sock, err = results.get(timeout=wait)
            except queue.Empty:
                continue
            inflight.remove(info)
            if sock is not None:
                winner = (info, sock)
            else:
                errors.append((info, err))
                next_launch = time.monotonic()  # RFC 8305 s5: a failed attempt releases the next at once
    finally:
        with lock:
            settled.append(True)
            while True:
                try:
                    _info, late, _err = results.get_nowait()
                except queue.Empty:
                    break
                if late is not None:
                    late.close()

    if winner is None:
        errors.extend((info, "timed out waiting for the connect to complete") for info in inflight)
        tried = "; ".join("%s %s: %s" % (_FAMILY_NAME.get(info[0], "?"), info[4][0], err)
                          for info, err in errors)
        raise OSError("could not connect to %s:%s -- %s" % (host, port, tried))
    info, sock = winner
    sock.settimeout(timeout)
    emit({"event": "connect", "family": _FAMILY_NAME.get(info[0], "?"),
          "ms": int(round((time.monotonic() - started) * 1000)),
          "dns_ms": int(round((resolved - started) * 1000)),
          "attempts": launched, "for": label})
    return sock


def _connect(parsed, timeout=CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S, label="request"):
    if parsed.scheme == "https":
        conn = http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed.hostname, parsed.port or 443,
                                            context=ssl.create_default_context(), timeout=timeout)
    else:
        conn = http.client.HTTPConnection(parsed.hostname, parsed.port or 80, timeout=timeout)
    # http.client opens its socket through this per-instance attribute (CPython
    # keeps socket.create_connection there "to allow unit tests to replace it"),
    # and HTTPSConnection then wraps whatever socket results with
    # server_hostname=conn.host -- the relay's NAME, never whichever address
    # happened to win -- so SNI and certificate verification are untouched.
    conn._create_connection = functools.partial(_create_connection, label=label)
    return conn


def _base_path(parsed):
    return parsed.path.rstrip("/")


def _body_head(raw, limit=200):
    try:
        text = raw.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    except Exception:
        text = repr(raw)
    return text[:limit]


def _keep_alive(sock):
    """TCP keepalive on a persistent frame connection's socket (KEEPALIVE_* above).
    Which knobs exist differs by platform -- TCP_KEEPIDLE on Linux, TCP_KEEPALIVE
    on macOS -- so each is set only where the socket module has it. A refusal is
    reported, never fatal: the connection works without it, just less robustly."""
    try:
        sock.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_KEEPALIVE, 1)
        for name, value in (("TCP_KEEPIDLE", KEEPALIVE_IDLE_S), ("TCP_KEEPALIVE", KEEPALIVE_IDLE_S),
                            ("TCP_KEEPINTVL", KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL_S), ("TCP_KEEPCNT", KEEPALIVE_PROBES)):
            option = getattr(socket, name, None)
            if option is not None:
                sock.setsockopt(socket.IPPROTO_TCP, option, value)
    except OSError as e:
        emit({"event": "error", "detail": "TCP keepalive not set on a frames connection: %s" % e})


class _FramesChannel:
    """One persistent HTTP/1.1 connection for POST /session/:id/frames, behind its
    own lock. FRAME CONNECTIONS in the module docstring says why, and spells out
    every rule below."""

    def __init__(self, parsed):
        self.parsed = parsed
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self.conn = None

    def _drop(self):
        conn, self.conn = self.conn, None
        if conn is not None:
            conn.close()

    def post(self, path, headers, body, timeout):
        """POST `body` and return (status, raw response body). `timeout` is ONE
        budget for the connect, the request, the response and the single retry on
        a replaced connection. Raises what http.client or the socket raised, after
        closing the connection that raised it."""
        with self.lock:
            started = time.monotonic()
            deadline = started + timeout
            retried = False
            while True:
                if self.conn is None:
                    self.conn = _connect(self.parsed, timeout=timeout, label="frames")
                conn = self.conn
                reused = conn.sock is not None
                conn.timeout = max(deadline - time.monotonic(), 0.001)
                try:
                    if reused:
                        conn.sock.settimeout(conn.timeout)
                    else:
                        conn.connect()
                        _keep_alive(conn.sock)
                    conn.request("POST", path, body=body, headers=headers)
                    resp = conn.getresponse()
                    raw = resp.read()
                except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as e:
                    self._drop()
                    if not reused or retried or isinstance(e, socket.timeout) or time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                        raise
                    retried = True  # a stale reused connection: the same envelope, once more, on a fresh one
                    continue
                except BaseException:
                    self._drop()  # whatever interrupted it, never leave a half-used connection to be reused
                    raise
                emit({"event": "post", "ms": int(round((time.monotonic() - started) * 1000)), "reused": reused})
                return resp.status, raw


class RelayClient:
    def __init__(self, args):
        self.args = args
        self.relay_url = args.relay
        self.parsed = _split_relay(args.relay)
        self.root = os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser(args.repo))
        self.status_path = args.status_file or os.path.join(self.root, ".heimdall", "app", "relay.json")
        self.tick_s = args.tick_s
        # A relay session is public by construction (state leaves the machine) --
        # public_host is always truthy so sentinels/hmd-ui.py's own public-mode
        # redaction (_redact_state_for_public) always applies, regardless of
        # whether the operator supplied a friendly --public-host name.
        self.public_host = (args.public_host.strip().lower() if args.public_host else "relay")
        self.transport = {"bind": "relay", "public_host": self.public_host,
                           "trust_proxy": False, "port": args.ui_port}

        self.session_id = None
        self.token = None
        self.exp = None
        self.priv = None
        self.pub = None
        self.session_key = None

        # `lock` covers seq allocation + sealing and the bookkeeping after a POST --
        # never a POST itself. Each sender POSTs on a connection of its own, behind
        # that connection's own lock (see FRAME CONNECTIONS), and `_acks_idle` is
        # signalled whenever an ack leaves the air (see send_hmd_frame).
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self._acks_in_flight = 0
        self._acks_idle = threading.Condition(self.lock)
        self._frame_channels = {"state": _FramesChannel(self.parsed), "ack": _FramesChannel(self.parsed)}
        # hmd's own first sealed frame now starts at seq=1 (not 0), and the
        # floor for an accepted device seq is 0 (not -1): both legs of the
        # app's crypto.ts enforce "seq must be strictly greater than 0" via
        # open()'s `seq <= lastSeq` check (INITIAL_SEQ = 1), so a 0-valued
        # first frame from either side was being silently rejected as a
        # replay -- audit #3, 2026-09-25.
        self.hmd_seq = 1
        self.last_device_seq = 0
        self.device_pub = None  # raw 32-byte key of the currently-bound
                                 # device -- set once, on the first
                                 # device_bound; see _handle_envelope.
        self.device_caps = frozenset()  # the phone's capability tokens: its latest
                                         # sealed `resync`'s caps (_resync), forgotten on
                                         # every device_bound; empty = plain frames only
        self.last_sent_state = None     # the state object of the last state frame sealed --
                                         # what a phone's resync digest is compared with
        self.stop_event = threading.Event()
        self.state_ready = threading.Event()
        self._stopping = False

        self.started_at = time.time()
        self.paired = False
        self.bound_at = None
        self.last_seq = None
        self.frames_sent = 0
        self.last_delivered = None
        self.last_command_at = None

        self.last_sent_digest = None
        self._rearm_gen = 0        # bumped by _rearm_state; see _tick_once
        self._state_failures = 0   # consecutive state POSTs that got no usable answer
        self.cache = None
        self._login = None         # the LoginManager, created on first use (see _login_manager)

    # -- status ------------------------------------------------------------
    def _status_snapshot(self):
        return {
            "session_id": self.session_id,
            "relay": self.relay_url,
            "paired": self.paired,
            "bound_at": self.bound_at,
            "last_seq": self.last_seq,
            "frames_sent": self.frames_sent,
            "last_delivered": self.last_delivered,
            "last_command_at": self.last_command_at,
            "started_at": self.started_at,
            "pid": os.getpid(),
            "event_log": _event_log_path,
        }

    def write_status(self):
        try:
            _atomic_write_json(self.status_path, self._status_snapshot())
        except OSError as e:
            # bookkeeping only -- surfaced, never fatal to the caller
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "status write failed: %s" % e})

    # -- HTTP: one-shot requests --------------------------------------------
    def _request(self, method, path, headers=None, body=None, timeout=CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S, label="request"):
        conn = _connect(self.parsed, timeout=timeout, label=label)
        try:
            conn.request(method, _base_path(self.parsed) + path, body=body, headers=headers or {})
            resp = conn.getresponse()
            raw = resp.read()
            return resp.status, raw
        finally:
            conn.close()

    def pair_init(self):
        try:
            status, raw = self._request("POST", "/pair/init", headers={"Content-Length": "0"}, body=b"",
                                         label="pair")
        except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as e:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "pair/init unreachable: %s" % e})
            return 12
        if status != 200:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "pair/init HTTP %d: %s" % (status, _body_head(raw))})
            return 12
        try:
            data = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8"))
        except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "pair/init returned invalid JSON: %s" % e})
            return 12
        for key in ("session_id", "pairing_code", "relay_session_token", "exp"):
            if key not in data:
                emit({"event": "error", "detail": "pair/init response missing %r" % key})
                return 12
        self.session_id = data["session_id"]
        self.token = data["relay_session_token"]
        self.exp = data["exp"]
        qr = {
            "v": 1,
            "relay": self.relay_url,
            "session_id": self.session_id,
            "pairing_code": data["pairing_code"],
            "exp": self.exp,
            "hmd_pubkey": E2E.pub_b64(self.pub),
        }
        emit({"event": "pair_init", "qr": qr, "exp": self.exp})
        self.write_status()
        return 0

    def _auth_headers(self, extra=None):
        h = {"Authorization": "Bearer %s" % self.token}
        if extra:
            h.update(extra)
        return h

    def send_frame_envelope(self, type_, nonce, ciphertext, seq, timeout=CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S):
        envelope = {
            "v": 1,
            "session_id": self.session_id,
            "seq": seq,
            "sender": "hmd",
            "type": type_,
            "nonce": nonce,
            "ciphertext": ciphertext,
            "payload": None,
        }
        body = json.dumps(envelope, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":")).encode("utf-8")
        if len(body) > MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "%s frame seq=%d dropped: %d bytes exceeds %d cap"
                                              % (type_, seq, len(body), MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES)})
            return None, len(body)
        # an ack and a state frame each have a connection (and lock) of their own
        channel = self._frame_channels["ack" if type_ == "ack" else "state"]
        try:
            status, raw = channel.post(_base_path(self.parsed) + "/session/%s/frames" % self.session_id,
                                       headers=self._auth_headers({"Content-Type": "application/json",
                                                                   "Connection": "keep-alive"}),
                                       body=body, timeout=timeout)
        except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as e:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "%s frame seq=%d post failed: %s" % (type_, seq, e)})
            return None, len(body)
        if status == 413:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "%s frame seq=%d rejected 413 (>%d bytes)"
                                              % (type_, seq, MAX_ENVELOPE_BYTES)})
            return None, len(body)
        if status != 200:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "%s frame seq=%d post HTTP %d: %s"
                                              % (type_, seq, status, _body_head(raw))})
            return None, len(body)
        try:
            resp = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8"))
        except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            resp = {}
        return bool(resp.get("delivered")), len(body)

    def send_hmd_frame(self, type_, plaintext_obj, ack_deadline=None):
        """Allocate the next shared hmd-side seq, seal, POST, update status --
        the ONE path both the state-tick loop (main thread) and the
        stream-reader thread use to send anything as sender 'hmd', so the seq
        counter (INV-14, strictly increasing per sender) is never raced
        between the two. An `ack` frame is POSTed by _post_ack, which retries a
        failed POST; `ack_deadline` (time.monotonic()) is the moment past which
        none of those retries may START (default: ACK_WINDOW_S from now).

        Only the seq allocation and the seal run under self.lock; the POST runs
        outside it, on the sender's own connection (FRAME CONNECTIONS), so a slow
        state POST never delays an ack. Ordering: a state frame waits for an ack
        in flight to finish before it takes its seq, so an ack -- retries
        included -- is never overtaken by a newer frame; the converse (a state
        frame still in flight when an ack takes the next seq) is _tick_once's
        to repair."""
        if type_ == "state":  # hmd's capability tokens ride in EVERY state frame (FRAME COMPRESSION AND RESYNC)
            plaintext_obj = dict(plaintext_obj, caps=E2E.hmd_caps(self._feature_caps(), push=PUSH_STORE is not None))
            self.last_sent_state = plaintext_obj.get("state")
        plaintext = json.dumps(plaintext_obj, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":")).encode("utf-8")
        if type_ == "state":  # compress BEFORE the seal; only a state frame, only for a phone that listed "z-zlib"
            plaintext = E2E.pack_plaintext(plaintext, self.device_caps)
        is_ack = type_ == "ack"
        with self.lock:
            if not is_ack:
                while self._acks_in_flight:
                    self._acks_idle.wait()
            seq = self.hmd_seq
            self.hmd_seq += 1
            nonce, ciphertext = E2E.seal(self.session_key, seq, "hmd", plaintext)
            if is_ack:
                self._acks_in_flight += 1
        try:
            if is_ack:
                delivered, nbytes = self._post_ack(plaintext_obj, nonce, ciphertext, seq, ack_deadline)
            else:
                delivered, nbytes = self.send_frame_envelope(type_, nonce, ciphertext, seq)
        finally:
            if is_ack:
                with self.lock:
                    self._acks_in_flight -= 1
                    self._acks_idle.notify_all()
        with self.lock:
            self.last_seq = seq if self.last_seq is None else max(self.last_seq, seq)
            if delivered is not None:
                self.frames_sent += 1
                self.last_delivered = delivered
            self.write_status()
        if type_ == "state":
            emit({"event": "state_sent", "seq": seq, "bytes": nbytes, "delivered": bool(delivered)})
        return seq, delivered

    def _post_ack(self, ack, nonce, ciphertext, seq, deadline):
        """POST one sealed ack frame, re-POSTing it if the POST FAILED (no usable
        answer) or was answered delivered=false (see ACK RETRY in the module
        docstring for every rule below), and emit one `ack_sent` event per
        attempt. Always the SAME envelope: the caller sealed it once, so every
        attempt carries the one seq the app dedupes on. Runs outside self.lock;
        send_hmd_frame counts this ack as in flight until it returns, and no
        state frame takes a seq meanwhile -- one sent in between would carry a
        higher seq, and the app drops any frame whose seq a newer one has
        already passed."""
        if deadline is None:
            deadline = time.monotonic() + ACK_WINDOW_S
        attempts = len(ACK_RETRY_DELAYS_S) + 1
        for attempt in range(1, attempts + 1):
            began = time.monotonic()
            # attempt 1 is always made, with the full per-attempt timeout (a
            # late ack beats none); a retry's timeout is capped to the window
            # that is left
            timeout = ACK_ATTEMPT_TIMEOUT_S if attempt == 1 else \
                min(ACK_ATTEMPT_TIMEOUT_S, max(deadline - began, ACK_MIN_ATTEMPT_S))
            delivered, nbytes = self.send_frame_envelope("ack", nonce, ciphertext, seq, timeout=timeout)
            emit({"event": "ack_sent", "of_seq": ack.get("of_seq"), "seq": seq, "id": ack.get("id"),
                  "attempt": attempt, "ms": int(round((time.monotonic() - began) * 1000)),
                  "ok": delivered is not None, "delivered": bool(delivered)})
            if delivered or attempt == attempts:
                break  # delivered; or out of attempts. No usable answer (None) and delivered=false both retry
            delay = ACK_RETRY_DELAYS_S[attempt - 1]
            if time.monotonic() + delay >= deadline or self.stop_event.wait(delay):
                break  # no room left in the window, or the client is stopping
        return delivered, nbytes

    def revoke(self):
        if not self.session_id or not self.token:
            return
        try:
            self._request("POST", "/session/%s/revoke" % self.session_id,
                           headers=self._auth_headers({"Content-Length": "0"}), body=b"", label="revoke")
        except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as e:
            # best-effort shutdown courtesy call -- surfaced, never blocks process exit
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "revoke call failed: %s" % e})

    # -- stream --------------------------------------------------------------
    def _open_stream(self):
        conn = _connect(self.parsed, timeout=STREAM_TIMEOUT_S, label="stream")
        conn.request("GET", _base_path(self.parsed) + "/session/%s/stream" % self.session_id,
                      headers=self._auth_headers())
        resp = conn.getresponse()
        return conn, resp

    def _decide(self, params):
        """The phone's sealed DENY or STOP of a pending risky action (A4, deny-only round; the doc's
        POST /api/decide, carried as a relay command the way send-message is):
            command  {"action":"decide","params":{"id":"p-<8 hex>","decision":"deny"|"stop"}}
            ack ok   {"ok":true,"of_seq":N,"id":"p-<8 hex>","decision":"deny"|"stop"}
            ack no   {"ok":false,"of_seq":N,"detail":<code>}
        `deny` refuses the call; `stop` refuses it and ends the turn (bin/heimdall-phone-deny turns
        either into its PreToolUse output). Reached only by a frame that opened under the paired
        device's session key and passed the replay guard, so there is no other authentication to do.
        The store (companion_ui_decisions) owns every rule and its machine-readable refusal:
        bad-decision, unknown-id, allow-not-permitted, already-decided, expired -- the phone can only
        reduce what runs, "allow" (and "approve", and any other word) is refused for every request,
        and nothing is written on a refusal."""
        if DECISIONS is None:
            return False, "decisions-unavailable", {}
        fields = params if isinstance(params, dict) else {}
        try:
            result = DECISIONS.decide(self.root, fields.get("id"), fields.get("decision"))
        except DECISIONS.DecisionError as e:
            return False, e.code, {}
        except OSError:
            return False, "write-failed", {}
        return True, None, {"id": result["id"], "decision": result["decision"]}

    def _adopt_device_caps(self, raw):
        """Record the phone's capability tokens (E2E.normalize_caps: an array's strings, anything
        else the empty set) and log a `device_caps` event when the set CHANGES. Called only with the
        `caps` of a `resync` command that OPENED under the paired session key, or with None on a
        device_bound (the phone's tokens are forgotten there) -- never with anything out of the
        relay's plaintext frames (see FRAME COMPRESSION AND RESYNC). The set is replaced by one
        reference assignment, so the state loop's read of it in send_hmd_frame needs no lock: it
        sees the old or the new set, both valid."""
        caps = E2E.normalize_caps(raw)
        if caps != self.device_caps:
            had_login = LOGIN is not None and LOGIN.CAP_LOGIN in self.device_caps
            self.device_caps = caps
            emit({"event": "device_caps", "caps": sorted(caps)})
            if LOGIN is not None and LOGIN.CAP_LOGIN in caps and not had_login:
                self._rearm_state()  # state.login rides only for a phone that lists login-v1: send it now

    def _resync(self, params):
        """The phone's `resync` command, sent to an hmd that listed `resync` in its state frames'
        caps (hmdapp's docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-zero-lag-sync.md 5.3):
            command  {"action":"resync","params":{"last_seq":N,"digest":"<64 lowercase hex>","caps":[..]}}
            ack ok   {"ok":true,"of_seq":N}            ack no  {"ok":false,"of_seq":N,"detail":"bad-params"}
        Keeps `caps` as the phone's capability set (the "z-zlib" gate; each resync replaces it) and,
        when `digest` is not the digest of the state hmd last sent, forces a full state frame on the
        next tick -- the very re-send device_bound forces -- so it carries the next seq, compressed
        if the new caps allow. A phone that already holds what hmd last sent needs nothing: the
        ordinary tick delivers any newer state. A `last_seq` at or above hmd's next seq means the
        two have desynced (the app shows RELAY_DESYNC); surfaced here as an error event, the seq
        counter itself is never touched."""
        fields = params if isinstance(params, dict) else {}
        last_seq, digest = fields.get("last_seq"), fields.get("digest")
        if not (isinstance(last_seq, int) and not isinstance(last_seq, bool) and last_seq >= 0
                and isinstance(digest, str) and len(digest) == 64
                and all(c in "0123456789abcdef" for c in digest)):
            return False, "bad-params"
        self._adopt_device_caps(fields.get("caps"))
        if last_seq >= self.hmd_seq:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "resync: phone last_seq=%d is at or above hmd's next seq %d -- "
                                              "desynced" % (last_seq, self.hmd_seq)})
        sent = self.last_sent_state
        if sent is None or E2E.state_digest(sent) != digest:
            self._rearm_state()
        return True, None

    def _push_ready(self):
        """Push registration is on: the store loaded and the operator has not set HMD_PUSH=0."""
        return PUSH_STORE is not None and E2E.push_enabled()

    def _register_push(self, params):
        """The phone's sealed registration of its Expo push token, sent only to an hmd that listed `push-v1`
        (section 5.2 of hmdapp's docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-03-push-notifications.md):
            command  {"action":"register_push","params":{"v":1,"provider":"expo","token":"<ExpoPushToken>",
                      "platform":"ios"|"android","ref":"<16 hex>","label":"<1..24>","events":[..]}}
            ack ok   {"ok":true,"of_seq":N,"ref":"<16 hex>","events":[..sorted]}
            ack no   {"ok":false,"of_seq":N,"detail":<code>}
        An idempotent upsert. The store (companion_push_store) owns every field rule and its machine-readable
        refusal, in the spec's order: bad-version and bad-provider are decided here, then bad-token |
        bad-platform | bad-ref | bad-label | bad-events there; a field that is absent is the refusal its rule
        names. Nothing is written on a refusal. The token and the label go into the store and nowhere else:
        the ack repeats only `ref` and `events`, and no event line names a field of the registration."""
        if not self._push_ready():
            return False, "push-disabled", {}
        if not isinstance(params, dict):
            return False, "bad-params", {}
        version = params.get("v")
        if not (isinstance(version, int) and not isinstance(version, bool) and version == 1):
            return False, "bad-version", {}
        if params.get("provider") != "expo":
            return False, "bad-provider", {}
        for field, code in (("token", "bad-token"), ("platform", "bad-platform"), ("ref", "bad-ref"),
                            ("label", "bad-label"), ("events", "bad-events")):
            if params.get(field) is None:
                return False, code, {}
        try:
            entry = PUSH_STORE.register(self.root, params["token"], params["platform"], ref=params["ref"],
                                        label=params["label"], events=params["events"])
        except PUSH_STORE.PushStoreError as e:
            return False, e.code, {}
        except OSError:
            return False, "write-failed", {}
        return True, None, {"ref": entry["ref"], "events": entry["events"]}

    def _unregister_push(self, params):
        """The phone withdrawing its registration (spec 5.3), sent before it forgets the session:
            command  {"action":"unregister_push","params":{"ref":"<16 hex>"}}
            ack ok   {"ok":true,"of_seq":N,"ref":"<16 hex>","removed":true|false}
        Idempotent: a well-formed ref nothing is registered under is `removed:false`, not an error."""
        if not self._push_ready():
            return False, "push-disabled", {}
        if not isinstance(params, dict):
            return False, "bad-params", {}
        ref = params.get("ref")
        if ref is None:
            return False, "bad-ref", {}
        try:
            removed = PUSH_STORE.unregister(self.root, ref=ref)
        except PUSH_STORE.PushStoreError as e:
            return False, e.code, {}
        except OSError:
            return False, "write-failed", {}
        return True, None, {"ref": ref, "removed": removed}

    def _app_state(self, params):
        """The phone saying whether it is on screen (spec 5.4), on every change between the two states and once
        after each successful resync -- what lets the notification sender stay quiet for a phone that is
        looking at the session:
            command  {"action":"app_state","params":{"state":"active"|"background"}}
            ack ok   {"ok":true,"of_seq":N}
        Kept as foreground / background with the time it arrived, so a report that stops arriving ages."""
        if not self._push_ready():
            return False, "push-disabled", {}
        if not isinstance(params, dict):
            return False, "bad-params", {}
        state = params.get("state")
        if state not in ("active", "background"):
            return False, "bad-state", {}
        try:
            PUSH_STORE.set_app_state(self.root, state)
        except OSError:
            return False, "write-failed", {}
        return True, None, {}

    def _forget_push_registrations(self):
        """This session is latching its key: whatever push.json holds was registered through an EARLIER
        session's key (a registration needs one, and this process had none until this very frame), so it
        belongs to a phone this session has never paired with. Its tokens go and the app state it reported is
        forgotten. A repeat device_bound with the same key keeps the registrations, and a different key never
        gets here -- the latch refuses it first. Bookkeeping: a failure is reported, never a reason to refuse
        the pairing."""
        if PUSH_STORE is None:
            return
        try:
            held = PUSH_STORE.load(self.root)
            if held["tokens"] or held["app_state"] != "unknown":
                PUSH_STORE.remove_tokens(self.root, [entry["token"] for entry in held["tokens"]])
                PUSH_STORE.set_app_state(self.root, "unknown")
        except OSError as e:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "push registrations from an earlier session were not cleared: %s" % e})

    # -- remote Claude Code login (login-v1) ----------------------------------------------------------
    # hmdapp's docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-remote-cc-login.md. bin/lib/companion_cc_login.py owns the login
    # (the PTY, the URL allowlist, the account pin); this is only its wire: three sealed commands, the
    # state.login slice of the state frame, and the capability token.
    def _login_manager(self):
        """The one LoginManager of this client, created on first use: nothing is read or spawned before."""
        if self._login is None:
            self._login = LOGIN.LoginManager(LOGIN.heimdall_home(), dict(os.environ),
                                             emit=lambda obj: emit(obj), on_change=self._rearm_state)
        return self._login

    def _feature_caps(self):
        """Capability tokens of the optional features whose modules loaded (added to the codec's own)."""
        return [E2E.CAP_LOGIN] if LOGIN is not None else []

    def _login_command(self, action, params):
        """The phone's sealed login commands (spec 4.3):
            {"action":"login_start","params":{"kind":"claudeai"|"console"}}
                ack ok  {"ok":true,"of_seq":N,"id":"l-<8 hex>"[,"dup":true]}
            {"action":"login_code","params":{"id":..,"code":"<CODE#STATE>"}}
                ack ok  {"ok":true,"of_seq":N,"id":..,"phase":"verifying"}
            {"action":"login_cancel","params":{"id":..}}
                ack ok  {"ok":true,"of_seq":N,"id":..}  (unknown or finished id: ok + detail "unchanged")
            ack no      {"ok":false,"of_seq":N,"detail":<LoginError.code>}
        The final outcome is never in an ack (the exchange takes seconds): it arrives in state.login.result.
        The code is a secret: it goes from `params` straight to the manager -- never logged, evented, stored
        or echoed, and no error text here ever includes it (only exception TYPE names are reported)."""
        fields = params if isinstance(params, dict) else {}
        has_cap = LOGIN.CAP_LOGIN in self.device_caps
        try:
            manager = self._login_manager()
            if action == "login_start":
                result = manager.start(fields.get("kind"), caps_ok=has_cap)
            elif not has_cap:
                return False, "caps-missing", {}
            elif action == "login_code":
                result = manager.submit_code(fields.get("id"), fields.get("code"))
            else:
                result = manager.cancel(fields.get("id"))
        except LOGIN.LoginError as e:
            return False, e.code, {}
        except Exception as e:  # the type only: a message could hold what the phone sent
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "login command failed: %s" % type(e).__name__})
            return False, "internal-error", {}
        detail = result.pop("detail", None)
        return True, detail, result

    def _login_overlay(self, state):
        """`state` plus state.login for a phone that listed login-v1 -- as a copy, because `state` is the
        StateCache's own shared object. Applied before the seal, so last_sent_state (what a resync digest is
        compared with) is exactly what the phone holds."""
        if LOGIN is None or LOGIN.CAP_LOGIN not in self.device_caps:
            return state
        try:
            return dict(state, login=self._login_manager().snapshot())
        except Exception as e:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "state.login skipped: %s" % type(e).__name__})
            return state

    def _login_shutdown(self):
        """The live login (if any) ends `superseded` and its process group is killed."""
        if self._login is not None:
            self._login.shutdown("superseded")

    def login_startup(self):
        """At start: kill a `claude auth login` an earlier client left behind, then run the probe loop on a
        thread of its own (it does nothing while remote login is switched off at the laptop)."""
        if LOGIN is None:
            return
        manager = self._login_manager()
        manager.sweep_orphan()
        threading.Thread(target=manager.run_probe_loop, args=(self.stop_event,), name="login-probe",
                         daemon=True).start()

    def _handle_command(self, env):
        # The phone's ack window opened before this command reached us; retries
        # of its ack may only START inside what is left of it (ACK_WINDOW_S).
        ack_deadline = time.monotonic() + ACK_WINDOW_S
        seq = env.get("seq")
        if not isinstance(seq, int) or seq <= self.last_device_seq:
            emit({"event": "error", "detail": "non-increasing seq from device (got %r, last %r) -- rejected"
                                              % (seq, self.last_device_seq)})
            return
        try:
            plaintext = E2E.open_(self.session_key, seq, "device", env.get("nonce"), env.get("ciphertext"))
        except E2E.E2EError:
            # audit #5, 2026-09-25: a frame that fails to decrypt must NEVER
            # advance last_device_seq. The monotonic check above runs BEFORE
            # decrypt, so an attacker-controlled seq on a forged/corrupted
            # frame would otherwise permanently poison the replay guard and
            # silently reject every later genuine command at or below that
            # seq. Only a frame that actually decrypts moves the high-water
            # mark (below, on the success path).
            emit({"event": "command", "action": None, "ok": False, "detail": "decrypt-failed"})
            self.send_hmd_frame("ack", {"ok": False, "detail": "decrypt-failed", "of_seq": seq},
                                ack_deadline=ack_deadline)
            return
        self.last_device_seq = seq
        self.last_command_at = time.time()
        try:
            obj = json.loads(plaintext.decode("utf-8"))
        except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            obj = None
        action = obj.get("action") if isinstance(obj, dict) else None
        params = obj.get("params") if isinstance(obj, dict) else None
        text = params.get("text") if isinstance(params, dict) else None
        extra = {}
        if action == "send-message":
            if INBOX is None:
                ok, detail = False, "inbox-unavailable"
            else:
                try:
                    record = INBOX.append(self.root, text)
                    ok, detail, extra = True, None, {"id": record["id"]}
                except INBOX.InboxError as e:
                    ok, detail = False, e.code
                except OSError:
                    ok, detail = False, "write-failed"
        elif action == "decide":
            ok, detail, extra = self._decide(params)
        elif LOGIN is not None and action in LOGIN.ACTIONS:
            ok, detail, extra = self._login_command(action, params)
        elif action == "resync":
            ok, detail = self._resync(params)
        elif action == "register_push":
            ok, detail, extra = self._register_push(params)
        elif action == "unregister_push":
            ok, detail, extra = self._unregister_push(params)
        elif action == "app_state":
            ok, detail, extra = self._app_state(params)
        else:
            ok, detail = False, "not-implemented"
        # action/ok/detail only, never `params`: a login_code command carries the login code (a secret)
        emit({"event": "command", "action": action, "ok": ok, "detail": detail})
        ack_payload = {"ok": ok, "of_seq": seq}
        if detail is not None:
            ack_payload["detail"] = detail
        ack_payload.update(extra)
        self.send_hmd_frame("ack", ack_payload, ack_deadline=ack_deadline)

    def _handle_envelope(self, env):
        sender = env.get("sender")
        type_ = env.get("type")
        if sender == "relay" and type_ == "device_bound":
            payload = env.get("payload") or {}
            try:
                device_pub = E2E.pub_from_b64(payload.get("device_pubkey") or "")
            except Exception as e:
                emit({"event": "error", "detail": "device_bound key derivation failed: %s" % e})
                return
            # §9, audit #1, 2026-09-25: the session key is latched at the FIRST
            # device_bound and never re-derived after. Re-deriving unconditionally
            # on every occurrence (the old behavior) meant a relay bug -- or a
            # malicious relay -- could rebind an already-paired session onto a
            # DIFFERENT device_pubkey with no visible error, silently handing the
            # new "device" decrypt access to future commands. A repeat with the
            # SAME device_pubkey (e.g. after a stream reconnect) is still the
            # expected, harmless case: no re-derivation needed (ECDH is
            # deterministic, so the result would be identical anyway), but §7c's
            # resync still has to fire, since the phone's local state was just
            # reset. A repeat with a DIFFERENT device_pubkey is never trusted or
            # adopted -- loud error, key and pairing state untouched.
            if self.session_key is not None:
                if device_pub == self.device_pub:
                    self._rearm_state()
                    self._adopt_device_caps(None)  # forget the phone's caps on device_bound: plain until its next resync
                    self.bound_at = payload.get("bound_at")
                    self.write_status()
                    emit({"event": "device_bound", "bound_at": self.bound_at})
                    return
                emit({"event": "error",
                      "detail": "device_bound device_pubkey differs from the already latched "
                                "session key's -- ignored, key not re-derived"})
                return
            try:
                self.session_key = E2E.derive_session_key(self.priv, device_pub, self.session_id)
            except Exception as e:
                emit({"event": "error", "detail": "device_bound key derivation failed: %s" % e})
                return
            self.device_pub = device_pub
            self.paired = True
            self.bound_at = payload.get("bound_at")
            self._forget_push_registrations()
            # §7c, 2026-09-25: force the next tick to re-send state even if its
            # digest hasn't changed since the last publish. A (re)bind means the
            # phone's own local state was just reset -- INV-22's digest dedup
            # must never suppress the resync it needs. `None` is the same
            # never-a-real-digest sentinel __init__ uses to guarantee the very
            # first tick always sends (line 256); digest_of() is a sha256 hex
            # digest and can never equal it.
            self._rearm_state()
            self.write_status()
            emit({"event": "device_bound", "bound_at": self.bound_at})
            self.state_ready.set()
            return
        if sender == "relay" and type_ == "session_ended":
            payload = env.get("payload") or {}
            self.write_status()
            emit({"event": "session_ended", "reason": payload.get("reason", "unknown")})
            self.stop_event.set()
            return
        if type_ == "keepalive":
            return
        if sender == "device" and type_ == "command":
            if self.session_key is None:
                emit({"event": "error", "detail": "command frame received before device_bound -- no session key yet, ignored"})
                return
            self._handle_command(env)
            return
        emit({"event": "error", "detail": "unexpected frame sender=%r type=%r -- ignored" % (sender, type_)})

    def _stream_loop(self):
        backoff_ms = BACKOFF_BASE_MS
        while not self.stop_event.is_set():
            try:
                conn, resp = self._open_stream()
            except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as e:
                emit({"event": "error", "detail": "stream connect failed: %s" % e})
                emit({"event": "stream_drop", "retry_ms": backoff_ms})
                if self.stop_event.wait(backoff_ms / 1000.0):
                    return
                backoff_ms = min(backoff_ms * 2, BACKOFF_CAP_MS)
                continue

            if resp.status in (401, 404, 410):
                self.write_status()
                emit({"event": "session_ended", "reason": "stream-%d" % resp.status})
                self.stop_event.set()
                conn.close()
                return

            if resp.status == 429:
                retry_after = resp.getheader("Retry-After")
                try:
                    wait_s = float(retry_after)
                except (TypeError, ValueError):
                    wait_s = backoff_ms / 1000.0
                conn.close()
                if self.stop_event.wait(wait_s):
                    return
                continue

            if resp.status != 200:
                emit({"event": "error", "detail": "stream open HTTP %d" % resp.status})
                emit({"event": "stream_drop", "retry_ms": backoff_ms})
                conn.close()
                if self.stop_event.wait(backoff_ms / 1000.0):
                    return
                backoff_ms = min(backoff_ms * 2, BACKOFF_CAP_MS)
                continue

            backoff_ms = BACKOFF_BASE_MS  # reset on a successful (re)connect
            drop_reason = None  # None == clean close, no other local cause
            overflow_info = None
            stream_total_bytes = 0
            rotate_deadline = time.monotonic() + STREAM_ROTATE_S
            try:
                while not self.stop_event.is_set():
                    remaining = rotate_deadline - time.monotonic()
                    if remaining <= 0:
                        drop_reason = "rotate"
                        break
                    conn.sock.settimeout(min(STREAM_IDLE_S, remaining))
                    line = resp.readline(STREAM_READ_CAP_BYTES)
                    if not line:
                        break
                    stream_total_bytes += len(line)
                    if (len(line) >= STREAM_READ_CAP_BYTES and not line.endswith(b"\n")) \
                            or stream_total_bytes > STREAM_READ_CAP_BYTES:
                        drop_reason = "overflow"
                        overflow_info = {"line_bytes": len(line), "total_bytes": stream_total_bytes,
                                          "cap_bytes": STREAM_READ_CAP_BYTES}
                        break
                    line = line.strip()
                    if not line:
                        continue
                    try:
                        env = json.loads(line.decode("utf-8"))
                    except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                        emit({"event": "error", "detail": "malformed frame from stream (not JSON)"})
                        continue
                    self._handle_envelope(env)
            except socket.timeout:
                drop_reason = "rotate" if time.monotonic() >= rotate_deadline else "idle"
            except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as e:
                emit({"event": "error", "detail": "stream read failed: %s" % e})
                drop_reason = "error"
            finally:
                conn.close()

            if self.stop_event.is_set():
                return

            if drop_reason == "rotate":
                emit({"event": "stream_drop", "retry_ms": 0, "reason": "rotate"})
                continue  # planned self-rotation -- no backoff, reopen immediately

            event = {"event": "stream_drop", "retry_ms": backoff_ms}
            if drop_reason == "overflow":
                event["reason"] = "overflow"
                event.update(overflow_info)
            elif drop_reason == "idle":
                event["reason"] = "idle"
            elif drop_reason is None:
                emit({"event": "error",
                      "detail": "stream closed by relay with no local cause (idle, "
                                "overflow and rotate all ruled out) -- likely the "
                                "relay's own stream-lifetime bound, possibly "
                                "following a relay deploy; reconnecting with backoff"})
                event["reason"] = "closed"
            emit(event)
            if self.stop_event.wait(backoff_ms / 1000.0):
                return
            backoff_ms = min(backoff_ms * 2, BACKOFF_CAP_MS)

    # -- state loop (main thread) --------------------------------------------
    # Send-on-change (docs/HANDOFF-TO-HEIMDALL-zero-lag-sync.md Ask 1): the loop blocks on
    # StateCache.wait_for_change, which the cache's poller wakes the moment the digest moves, instead
    # of sleeping --tick-s between looks -- so the frame follows the change, not a second 2 s timer.
    STATE_DEBOUNCE_S = 0.05      # coalesce a burst: send once the digest has been still this long ...
    STATE_DEBOUNCE_MAX_S = 0.25  # ... but never hold a burst's first change longer than this
    STATE_WAIT_SLICE_S = 0.25    # longest single block on the cache: a stop_event.set() or a
                                 # _rearm_state() (both on the stream thread) cannot wake it

    def _rearm_state(self):
        """A (re)bind: the phone's local state was just reset, so the next tick must
        send the state again whatever its digest -- the state loop re-reads
        last_sent_digest at least every STATE_WAIT_SLICE_S (see _await_new_state).
        The generation bump is how a state POST that is in flight right now can
        tell, once it is answered, that it must not write its digest over this
        (see _tick_once)."""
        self.last_sent_digest = None
        self._rearm_gen += 1

    def _tick_once(self):
        state, digest = self.cache.latest()
        if digest == self.last_sent_digest:
            return
        rearm_gen = self._rearm_gen
        seq, delivered = self.send_hmd_frame("state", {"state": self._login_overlay(state)})
        if delivered is None:
            # No usable answer: the frame never reached the phone. last_sent_digest
            # stays as it was, so the next tick sends the (then current) state again
            # -- after a bounded backoff, so a relay that is down is not hammered.
            delay = STATE_RETRY_BACKOFF_S[min(self._state_failures, len(STATE_RETRY_BACKOFF_S) - 1)]
            self._state_failures += 1
            self.stop_event.wait(delay)
            return
        self._state_failures = 0
        if self.hmd_seq - 1 > seq:
            # An ack took a higher seq while this frame was in flight and may have
            # landed first, and the phone drops a frame whose seq a newer one has
            # passed: this frame may be lost, so it is not recorded as sent.
            return
        if rearm_gen == self._rearm_gen:  # a rebind that landed mid-POST must survive it
            self.last_sent_digest = digest

    def _await_new_state(self):
        """Block until the cache holds a digest this client has not sent, then let a burst settle.
        True when there is something to send: the digest stayed put for STATE_DEBOUNCE_S, or kept
        moving until STATE_DEBOUNCE_MAX_S ran out. False when a wait slice passed with nothing new
        (or on stop) -- the caller loops, which is how stop_event and a _rearm_state() re-bind
        (last_sent_digest = None, re-read here on every call) are noticed within one slice. A state
        POST that got no usable answer leaves last_sent_digest as it was (_tick_once), so it reads as
        "new" again here and is retried after _tick_once's own backoff. --tick-s (default 2) only
        bounds the slice from above: nothing waits for it any more."""
        if self.stop_event.is_set():
            return False
        _, digest = self.cache.wait_for_change(self.last_sent_digest, min(self.tick_s, self.STATE_WAIT_SLICE_S))
        if digest is None or digest == self.last_sent_digest:
            return False
        began = time.monotonic()
        while not self.stop_event.is_set():
            hold = self.STATE_DEBOUNCE_MAX_S - (time.monotonic() - began)
            if hold <= 0:
                break
            _, newer = self.cache.wait_for_change(digest, min(self.STATE_DEBOUNCE_S, hold))
            if newer == digest:
                break
            digest = newer
        return not self.stop_event.is_set()

    def run_state_loop(self):
        self.cache = UI.StateCache(self.root, self.transport)
        self.cache.start()
        try:
            while not self.stop_event.is_set():
                try:
                    if not self.state_ready.is_set():
                        # nothing can be sealed before device_bound; setting the event wakes this at once
                        self.state_ready.wait(min(self.tick_s, self.STATE_WAIT_SLICE_S))
                    elif self._await_new_state():
                        self._tick_once()
                except Exception as e:
                    emit({"event": "error", "detail": "state tick failed: %s" % e})
                    self.stop_event.wait(self.tick_s)  # a failing pass is retried at the old cadence, never hot
        finally:
            self.cache.stop()

    # -- shutdown --------------------------------------------------------------
    def shutdown(self, reason):
        if self._stopping:
            return
        self._stopping = True
        self.stop_event.set()
        self._login_shutdown()  # before the (network-bound) revoke: a login process must not outlive us
        self.revoke()
        self.write_status()
        emit({"event": "session_ended", "reason": reason})


def build_argparser():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="heimdall-relay-client",
                                  description="publish hmd ui state through the Cloudflare relay, "
                                              "E2E sealed, accepting send-message and decide (deny-only) commands back")
    ap.add_argument("--relay", required=True, metavar="URL",
                     help="relay base URL, e.g. https://hmd-relay.example.workers.dev")
    ap.add_argument("--repo", required=True, metavar="DIR", help="repo root to render (passed to collect_state)")
    ap.add_argument("--ui-port", required=True, type=int, metavar="N",
                     help="port a local `hmd ui` is bound to (informational -- carried in the "
                          "transport dict, never dialed by this process)")
    ap.add_argument("--public-host", default=None, metavar="NAME",
                     help="friendly name shown in the transport dict; public-mode redaction is "
                          "always applied for a relay session regardless of this flag")
    ap.add_argument("--status-file", default=None, metavar="PATH",
                     help="default <repo>/.heimdall/app/relay.json")
    ap.add_argument("--tick-s", type=float, default=2.0, metavar="N",
                     help="longest the state loop blocks before it re-checks for a stop or a re-bind "
                          "(default 2, and never more than 0.25 -- STATE_WAIT_SLICE_S). A state change is "
                          "sent as it happens (debounced 50 ms), not at the next tick")
    return ap


def main(argv=None):
    ap = build_argparser()
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    parsed = _split_relay(args.relay)
    if parsed is None:
        ap.error("--relay must be an http:// or https:// URL")

    # Configured before the first possible emit() below (mirrors _split_relay
    # being computed here AND again inside RelayClient.__init__ -- a cheap
    # pure computation done early for a pre-flight need, same pattern) so
    # even the e2e-unavailable/UI-import error events are captured.
    configure_event_log(os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser(args.repo)))

    if not IP_FAMILY_VALID:
        emit({"event": "error",
              "detail": "HMD_RELAY_IP_FAMILY=%r is not one of auto|4|6 -- using auto" % _IP_FAMILY_ENV})

    if UI is None:
        emit({"event": "error",
              "detail": "cannot import sentinels/hmd-ui.py -- collect_state/digest_of/StateCache unavailable"})
        return 1

    e2e_ok = E2E is not None
    if e2e_ok:
        try:
            e2e_ok = bool(E2E.e2e_available())
        except Exception:
            e2e_ok = False
    if not e2e_ok:
        msg = "relay E2E unavailable -- refusing to start a plaintext relay session"
        emit({"event": "error", "detail": msg})
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-relay-client: %s\n" % msg)
        return 11

    client = RelayClient(args)
    client.priv, client.pub = E2E.generate_keypair()

    rc = client.pair_init()
    if rc != 0:
        return rc

    def _on_signal(_signum, _frame):
        client.shutdown("local-disconnect")
        sys.exit(0)

    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, _on_signal)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, _on_signal)

    client.login_startup()

    stream_thread =threading.Thread(target=client._stream_loop, name="relay-stream", daemon=True)
    stream_thread.start()

    client.run_state_loop()
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
